I would also use @ValueSource here.
So why does MethodSource exist?
Because the method can generate data instead of just storing it.
Example 1: Read from Excel
static Stream<String> searchData() {

return ExcelUtils.readExcel().stream();

}
Now you don't type anything.
Example 2: Read from Database
static Stream<String> searchData() {

return DatabaseUtils.getSearchData().stream();

}
Again, the test stays unchanged.
Example 3: Read from JSON
static Stream<String> searchData() {

return JsonReader.getData().stream();

}
Example 4: Generate data dynamically
Suppose today's date is required.
static Stream<String> searchData() {

return Stream.of(
LocalDate.now().toString(),
LocalDate.now().plusDays(1).toString()
);

}
You can't do this with @ValueSource.
Example 5: Create 100 inputs
static Stream<String> searchData() {

return IntStream.range(1,101)
.mapToObj(i -> "Employee " + i);
}
No need to manually type 100 values.
Another advantage: reusable data
Suppose three different test classes need the same search data.
With @ValueSource:
Test1
@ValueSource(...)

Test2
@ValueSource(...)

Test3
@ValueSource(...)
You duplicate the list three times.
With @MethodSource:
public class TestData {

static Stream<String> googleData() {
...
}
}
Every test simply does:
@MethodSource("utils.TestData#googleData")
Now there is only one place to maintain the data.
Rule of thumb
Use @ValueSource when:
One parameter
Small number of values
Data won't change
You're writing a quick test
Example:
@ValueSource(strings={
"Chrome",
"Firefox",
"Edge"
})
Use @MethodSource when:
Data comes from Excel
Data comes from CSV
Data comes from JSON
Data comes from a database
Data is generated at runtime
Multiple tests share the same dataset
The dataset is large or complex
My recommendation for your Serenity framework
Since you're building a framework to learn and potentially reuse, I'd practice all three approaches:
@ValueSource — Learn the basics of JUnit parameterized tests.
@MethodSource — Learn how JUnit separates the test from its data provider.
@MethodSource + Excel — This is the pattern you'll most often encounter in real-world automation frameworks, because changing test data doesn't require changing the test code.
So your intuition is correct: for a handful of hard-coded strings, @ValueSource is simpler and preferable. @MethodSource becomes valuable when the data provider does more than simply list values.

Does method source need steam only or it can be replaced?
Good question. No, @MethodSource does not require only Stream. It supports several return types.
According to JUnit 5, a method source can return:
Stream<T> ✅ (most common)
List<T> ✅
Set<T> ✅
Iterable<T> ✅
Iterator<T> ✅
Arrays (T[]) ✅
JUnit internally converts these into a stream of arguments.
1. Stream (Most Common)
static Stream<String> searchData() {
return Stream.of(
"Selenium",
"Serenity",
"Java"
);
}
2. List
static List<String> searchData() {
return Arrays.asList(
"Selenium",
"Serenity",
"Java"
);
}
Works exactly the same.
3. Array
static String[] searchData() {
return new String[]{
"Selenium",
"Serenity",
"Java"
};
}
Also works.
4. Set
static Set<String> searchData() {
return Set.of(
"Selenium",
"Serenity",
"Java"
);
}
5. Iterable
static Iterable<String> searchData() {

List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add("Selenium");
list.add("Serenity");
list.add("Java");
return list;
}
Which one should you use?
For a Serenity framework, I recommend:
If data comes from Excel
public static List<String> getSearchData() {
...
}
Then your method source can simply return the list:
@MethodSource("searchData")

static List<String> searchData() {
return ExcelUtils.getSearchData();
}
No need to convert it to a Stream.
Why do most examples use Stream?
Because Stream provides powerful operations such as:
Stream.of(...)
.filter(...)
.map(...)
.sorted()
.distinct();
For example, if your Excel contains blank rows:
static Stream<String> searchData() {

return ExcelUtils.getSearchData()
.stream()
.filter(s -> !s.isBlank());

}
This is one reason many examples prefer Stream—it lets you transform or filter the data before it reaches the test.
For your learning framework
Since you're reading from Excel into a List<String>, I'd actually write it like this:
public static List<String> getSearchData() {
...
}
and
@MethodSource("searchData")

static List<String> searchData() {
return ExcelUtils.getSearchData();
}
It's simpler, avoids an unnecessary .stream() call, and clearly expresses that you're just returning a collection of test data.

If using with excel i saw using stream

Yes, you'll often see Stream used with Excel examples, but that's because the Excel-reading method usually returns a List first, and then it's converted to a Stream. It's a choice, not a requirement.
For example:
public static List<String> getSearchData() {
List<String> data = new ArrayList<>();
// Read Excel and populate data
return data;
}
Option 1 (most common in tutorials)
@MethodSource("excelData")

static Stream<String> excelData() {
return ExcelUtils.getSearchData().stream();
}
Here:
Excel → List<String>
.stream() converts the list to a Stream<String>.
Option 2 (also valid)
@MethodSource("excelData")

static List<String> excelData() {
return ExcelUtils.getSearchData();
}
No stream() at all.
Why do people use Stream?
There are two main reasons:
JUnit documentation and examples primarily use Stream, so many tutorials follow that style.
It allows processing the data before passing it to the test. For example:
static Stream<String> excelData() {
return ExcelUtils.getSearchData()
.stream()
.filter(s -> !s.isBlank())
.map(String::trim);
}
This filters out blank rows and trims whitespace without modifying the Excel utility.
Which should you use?
If your Excel utility simply returns the rows and you don't need to transform them, this is perfectly fine:
static List<String> excelData() {
return ExcelUtils.getSearchData();
}
If later you need to filter, sort, or manipulate the data before the test runs, returning a Stream becomes more convenient.
So, for your current Google search example, both approaches are correct. The use of Stream in Excel examples is mostly a matter of flexibility and convention, not a requirement imposed by @MethodSource.

